            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Verdict: Account Tiering Is Non-Negotiable</h2>
<p>If your multi-account team is still managing all accounts with the same approach, you're likely burning budget unnecessarily. Account tiering isn't optional—it's the foundational efficiency system for multi-account operations. Without it, budget allocation lacks basis, risk control has no boundaries, and team collaboration devolves into reactive firefighting.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Why Tiering Matters: Three Critical Dimensions</h2>
<h3>1. Budget Allocation Requires Prioritization</h3>
<p>The most common operational issue: a team has 20 accounts but distributes budget entirely by intuition. When launching new channels or products, whichever account generates volume fastest gets more budget; underperforming accounts linger without shutdown, starving high-performing accounts of resources.</p>
<p>One utility app's paid media team reported managing 8 Facebook ad accounts, with just 3 delivering over 70% of conversions. The other 5 accounts had consistently below-average ROI but received no isolation treatment. The root cause was the absence of a tiering mechanism—budget was distributed evenly regardless of performance.</p>
<h3>2. Risk Isolation Determines Team Survival</h3>
<p>The biggest fear in international advertising? Account bans. If your primary account gets banned and all budget is concentrated there, your entire campaign schedule collapses. A core value of tiered management is containing "account explosion" losses to the minimum scope.</p>
<p>Recommendation: tier accounts by historical performance and risk level. High-risk accounts (frequent review triggers, newly opened accounts) are managed separately without sharing budget pools with primary accounts. Primary accounts get priority for stable spend with independent funding and alerting mechanisms.</p>
<h3>3. Team Collaboration Needs Clear Boundaries</h3>
<p>Without tiering, collaboration becomes an "account grab" game. Optimizer A and Optimizer B might simultaneously adjust the same account's bid, overwriting each other's work. Or new operators are assigned to core accounts for trial runs, causing primary account volatility.</p>
<p>After tiering, each optimizer has clear performance metrics and operational permissions for their assigned account tier, naturally improving collaboration efficiency.</p>
<hr />
<h2>A Practical Three-Tier Framework</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tier</th>
<th>Account Characteristics</th>
<th>Management Strategy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Primary</strong></td>
<td>Stable spend, above-average ROI, clean history</td>
<td>Priority budget allocation, experienced staff, regular data reviews</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Growth</strong></td>
<td>Growth potential but unstable performance, testing new creatives or audiences</td>
<td>Medium budget, focused conversion path observation, defined stop-loss periods</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Isolated</strong></td>
<td>New accounts, high-risk operations, history of bans</td>
<td>Independent budget pool, spending caps, no association with primary accounts</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Tiering isn't a one-time task. Conduct weekly account performance reviews and adjust tier assignments based on data.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Common Pitfalls to Avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Overcomplicated tiering criteria</strong>: If your tiering logic requires 30 minutes to explain, it won't stick in practice. Simplify to three tiers: Primary, Growth, and Isolated.</li>
<li><strong>Tiering without management</strong>: Without corresponding strategies and staffing, tiering is just paperwork.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring funding rhythms</strong>: Separate funding channels for primary and isolated accounts to prevent financial reconciliation confusion.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>The Takeaway: Tiering Is Leverage, Not Burden</h2>
<p>Account tiering fundamentally concentrates limited operational energy and budget resources on the most valuable accounts. It doesn't increase management complexity—it reduces decision costs. When you can clearly answer "What's our primary account spend ratio this week? How are the new account tests progressing?" your multi-account operation truly enters a controllable state.</p>
